# Cursor Rules for Meshflow/StudyMesh

## Project Overview
This is a Next.js 16 application (App Router) for intelligent B2B team enablement and matching. The project uses TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Shadcn UI, Supabase, and Drizzle ORM.

## Tech Stack
- **Framework**: Next.js 16 (App Router)
- **Language**: TypeScript (strict mode)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS + Shadcn UI + Framer Motion
- **Backend**: Supabase (Auth + Postgres)
- **ORM**: Drizzle ORM
- **Matching Logic**: Custom TypeScript fuzzy matching engine

## Code Style & Conventions
- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Follow Next.js App Router conventions
- Use functional components with React hooks
- Prefer server components by default, use 'use client' only when needed
- Use async/await for async operations
- Follow Shadcn UI component patterns
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling (no inline styles)
- Use Framer Motion for animations

## File Structure
- `src/app/` - Next.js App Router pages and layouts
- `src/components/` - Reusable React components
- `src/lib/` - Utility functions, database, and business logic
- `src/hooks/` - Custom React hooks
- `drizzle/` - Database migrations

## Key Patterns
- Authentication: Supabase Auth with SSR
- Database: Drizzle ORM with PostgreSQL (Supabase)
- Matching: Fuzzy string matching for skill-based matching
- UI: Shadcn UI components with Tailwind CSS
- Animations: Framer Motion for smooth transitions

## Best Practices
- Always handle errors gracefully
- Use TypeScript types strictly (avoid `any`)
- Optimize for server-side rendering when possible
- Use Supabase RLS (Row Level Security) for data protection
- Follow Next.js 16 best practices for data fetching
- Keep components small and focused
- Use proper TypeScript types from Drizzle schema

## When Writing Code
- Write clean, maintainable, and well-typed code
- Add proper error handling
- Consider performance implications
- Follow the existing code patterns in the project
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Add comments for complex logic




